      "body": "Hi {{contact.first_name}},\n\nOne of the first things we did for a new venue client this summer was lower her ad budget.\n\nI know. That's not usually what you'd expect to hear from an ads agency.\n\nOn one of our very first strategy calls, we asked how she felt about her current spend, and her answer was one ads agencies don't usually love hearing. It had crept up over the year over the past year, her website traffic was up, and she wasn't seeing stronger leads to show for it. She'd love to bring it down.\n\nHonestly, that's a great instinct. Our answer was yes, just not all at once.\n\nSo before we did anything else, we looked at what her money was actually doing.\n\nHer Google ads were performing well. But when a budget keeps climbing, Google can sometimes start to assume you're okay paying more for every lead. Cutting it to a fraction overnight would have been too drastic, so we brought it down to about half and started a fresh campaign to see what her leads should really cost.\n\nOn Meta, the campaign spending the most hadn't produced a single tracked lead. It was trying to find all of her new inquiries among people who had visited her website in the last 45 days, which is a very small group to ask that much of. We turned it off before the call was over. It doesn't make sense to spend just to spend.\n\nThen we rebuilt her Meta ads at a lower total budget and gave each campaign one clear job. One introduces the venue to couples who've never heard of it. One asks for the inquiry. And one stays in front of couples who are still deciding.\n\nShe also told us which nearby cities tend to send her price shoppers, so we left those out and pointed her ads toward the places her ideal couples actually come from.\n\nFrom there, the plan is simple. Start lower, find out what's genuinely working, and then scale it.\n\nTo be clear, I'm a big believer in investing in growth. I've seen what happens when wedding pros stop tinkering and fully commit to their advertising.\n\nBut \"spend more\" is the most common advice in our industry, and the ad platforms will always suggest a bigger daily budget. A bigger budget amplifies whatever is already there. If a campaign isn't doing its job, more money just means it spends more, faster.\n\nYour budget should follow the proof.\n\nThat's how we think about budget with our clients, whether we're managing their ads through ELEVATE, our full-service advertising management program, or launching their campaigns for them and guiding them while they run them through our Ads Accelerators.\n\nSo here's my question for you. Is your ad budget higher than it was a year ago? And are your bookings?\n\nHit reply and tell me. I'd love to hear what you're seeing.\n\nTalk soon,\nWhitney",
